---
description: Project-wide conventions for the SaaS NestJS backend
alwaysApply: true
---

## Stack
- Node.js ≥ 20, pnpm ≥ 9 (never use npm/yarn commands or lockfiles)
- NestJS 11 with Fastify adapter (not Express) — use Fastify-compatible packages only
- PostgreSQL + Prisma ORM (no TypeORM/Sequelize patterns)
- TypeScript strict mode for all new code
- Custom JWT/bcrypt auth — do NOT introduce Passport.js or other auth middleware

## Dependency management
- Always use `pnpm add` / `pnpm add -D` — never edit package.json manually then run install
- Use `-D` for anything that's build/test/lint tooling only; runtime deps go without the flag
- Check if a capability already exists in the codebase (common/, existing modules) before adding a new package

## Imports
- Use relative imports within a module/feature folder (e.g. `./dto/login.dto`)
- Use barrel exports (`index.ts`) per feature folder to expose its public surface; import from the barrel outside the folder, not deep-imported internal files
- Never import Prisma-generated types directly into controllers — go through DTOs

## Code style
- Full TypeScript types on all function params, return types, and class properties — no implicit `any`
- Prefer `interface` for object shapes, `type` for unions/utility types
- Async/await only — no raw `.then()` chains
- Errors are always typed exceptions (NestJS HttpException subclasses), never thrown strings/objects

## Response shape
- All API responses use the project's standard envelope (see `common/`) — do not return raw Prisma objects or ad hoc shapes
- Version all new routes under `/api/v1` (or current version) per existing convention

## Environment & secrets
- Never hardcode secrets, tokens, or credentials — read via a validated config service
- Never commit `.env*`, seeded passwords, OTPs, recovery links, or refresh tokens
- Any new required env var must be added to `.env.example` and validated at startup (fail fast, not silent fallback)